{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>No manner of work shall be done on them</b> — Rashi explained: even through others. And I do not understand this, for if these others are Israelites, they themselves are warned concerning it, and I am not warned about my work that it not be done by him, except that if I mislead him in a prohibition, I am warned on account of 'before the blind you shall not place a stumbling block' (Leviticus 19:14) — whether in his work or in the work of the one who acts on his own behalf. And if these others are Gentiles, we are not warned by the Torah concerning a Gentile's work at all, neither on a Festival nor on the Sabbath, except that it involves a shevut by rabbinic decree together with our instructing him, as they said: 'instructing a Gentile is a shevut' (Shabbat 150a). And this matter is explicit in the Gemara.\n\nBut I found in the Mekhilta (here): '“No manner of work shall be done on them” — you shall not do it, and your fellow shall not do it, and a Gentile shall not do your work.' Do I mean: you shall not do it and your fellow shall not do it and a Gentile shall not do your work? Or perhaps: you shall not do it and your fellow shall not do it and a Gentile shall not do his own work? Scripture teaches: 'Six days shall work be done' (Leviticus 23:3) — thus, you shall not do it and your fellow shall not do it, but a Gentile may do his own work. These are the words of Rabbi Yosiah. And Rabbi Yonatan says: this is not needed, for was it not already stated (Exodus 20:9): 'Six days shall you labor and do all your work' — and the matter follows an a fortiori argument: if for the Sabbath, which is more stringent, you are not warned concerning a Gentile's work as you are concerning your own, etc. — end of this baraita. And it is certainly a mere asmakhta, for they came to prohibit by rabbinic decree instructing a Gentile regarding our work, and they supported it from this verse; but a Gentile's work on his own behalf is permitted. And according to our approach, we have learned that they did not decree concerning instructing a Gentile except regarding our work, but regarding his own, one may say to a Gentile and he does it. And thus Rashi explained in the Gemara in Bava Metzia (90a). And there in the Mekhilta there is more: 'I know only matters that constitute work; matters that are a shevut, from where do I know them? Scripture teaches: “And you shall observe this day” (Exodus 12:17), to include matters that are a shevut.' And baraitot such as these are misleading and it is not proper to write them according to their plain sense, for this too appears to be a mere asmakhta. And I have a correct interpretation of this matter; I shall yet speak of it, with the help of Heaven (in Leviticus 23:24)." "<b>To every soul</b> — even the soul of an animal. Could it also include Gentiles? Scripture teaches: 'to you' — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 12:16). And this too is not the halakhah. Rather: 'to you' and not to Gentiles; 'to you' and not to an animal (see Beitza 21a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviation בע\"ה is resolved as בעזרת השם, 'with the help of Heaven.' Ramban cross-references his forthcoming discussion at Leviticus 23:24."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:27.739186150Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}